WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.510
Imagine writing two programs, running them
side by side, and asking them to look at

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.480
the exact same memory address.

3
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.220
You would expect them to
find the exact same data.

4
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:15.550
But on a modern operating
system, they don't.

5
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.600
They find completely different values.

6
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.700
This happens because of a core
deception in the operating system.

7
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.090
On an x86-64 Linux machine, the
system lies to every single process.

8
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:32.010
It gives each program the illusion that
it is running alone, inside a massive

9
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.480
private address space of 256 terabytes.

10
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.390
This terminal output from our course lab
shows exactly how a process divides that

11
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.570
private space. We can see distinct zones
for the code, the initialized data, the

12
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:50.390
heap for small memory requests, the mmap
regions for large ones, and the stack for

13
00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.790
local variables. But those
addresses are strictly virtual.

14
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.680
They do not point directly to the
physical RAM chips on your motherboard.

15
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.110
By giving each program its own fake coordinate
system, the kernel perfectly isolates

16
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:08.250
them. No program can accidentally overwrite
another's data, because they aren't even

17
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.280
speaking the same physical language.

18
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.610
To bridge this gap, virtual memory is broken
into chunks called pages, each exactly

19
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:21.980
4,096 bytes long. Physical RAM is
divided into matching 4,096-byte frames.

20
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.210
A kernel-maintained page table, helped by
the hardware memory management unit and

21
00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:31.280
TLB cache, actively routes every virtual
page to a specific physical frame.

22
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.070
When a program tries to use a virtual
page that doesn't have a physical frame

23
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.700
assigned yet, the hardware
pauses the process.

24
00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.580
It raises what is called a page fault,
and jumps down into the kernel.

25
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.570
The kernel then grabs a free physical frame,
clears it out, updates the page table,

26
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.040
and retries the instruction.

27
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.270
This is called lazy allocation.

28
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.390
The system only spends physical memory
at the exact moment the program actually

29
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.210
touches it. This strict, hardware-assisted
mapping layer is what makes the whole

30
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:04.170
illusion work. Because the kernel controls
the translations, a process is physically

31
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.180
trapped inside its own pages.

32
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:11.690
So what happens to this massive, carefully
mapped address space when a process needs

33
00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.460
to clone itself using
the fork system call?

34
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.100
Copying all that data physically
would be incredibly slow.

35
00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.380
But look at this lab output.

36
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:27.060
Cloning a 256-megabyte process on the
course machine took just 6.70 milliseconds.

37
00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:31.400
That speed is physically impossible if
the RAM was actually being duplicated.

38
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.020
The system achieves this through
a trick called copy-on-write.

39
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.780
Instead of copying the actual memory,
the kernel only copies the page tables.

40
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:45.060
It points both processes to the exact same
physical frames, marking them read-only.

41
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.930
If one modifies the data, the kernel
intercepts the write, duplicates that specific

42
00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.860
4096-byte page, and lets
the program continue.

43
00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.160
This takes only 2,753
nanoseconds per fault.

44
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:02.850
Copy-on-write turns a massive memory
duplication job into a lazy, on-demand

45
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:07.510
operation. It saves time and RAM, while
keeping the illusion of isolated memory

46
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.790
completely intact. But absolute
cloning has a strange side effect.

47
00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.650
If you use a function like printf right
before calling fork, you might see your text

48
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.760
print twice on the screen,
like in this lab output.

49
00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.740
The C library doesn't
output text immediately.

50
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:27.220
It accumulates it in an internal buffer
inside the process's user-mode memory.

51
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:32.050
When fork executes, it copies the entire
memory space perfectly, including that

52
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:37.270
unflushed buffer. Now, both parent and
child hold the exact same pending text in

53
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.850
memory. When both processes finish and
call the library exit function, they both

54
00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.880
flush their buffers to the
screen, printing the line twice.

55
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.910
If the parent had called the underscore
exit system call instead, it would have

56
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.240
skipped the flush and
avoided the duplicate.

57
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.600
The system's dedication to
the illusion is absolute.

58
00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:59.350
The kernel ensures that every single byte
of the process is flawlessly cloned, even

59
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.120
its buffered mistakes. Let's review.

60
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.380
Question one, why is fork so
fast, even for a large process?

61
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:13.420
Because of copy-on-write. It only copies
the page tables, not the physical memory.

62
00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:19.360
Question two, why might printed text
appear twice after calling fork?

63
00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:25.030
Because the C library buffer is inside
the process memory, and fork copies the

64
00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.020
unflushed buffer to the child.

65
00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:34.250
Now open animations A4.1, A4.2,
and A4.6 in the course hub, and run

66
00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.020
lab TD3.
